WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.199
Allocation safety depends on more than calling the right allocation mechanism.

2
00:00:04.199 --> 00:00:07.635
Size values need to be trusted only after validation.

3
00:00:07.635 --> 00:00:14.507
Counts, lengths, dimensions, element sizes, and serialized fields should be checked before they influence allocation, indexing, copying, or

4
00:00:14.507 --> 00:00:14.889
construction.

5
00:00:14.889 --> 00:00:16.798
Size arithmetic deserves explicit review.

6
00:00:16.798 --> 00:00:25.197
Multiplication, addition, and unit conversion can exceed the representable range or produce a value that does not match the intended object layout.

7
00:00:25.197 --> 00:00:29.778
Check arithmetic before allocating, especially when external input controls counts or lengths.

8
00:00:29.778 --> 00:00:32.832
Prefer containers over manually allocated arrays when possible.

9
00:00:32.832 --> 00:00:36.650
A container keeps size, capacity, ownership, and cleanup behavior together.

10
00:00:36.650 --> 00:00:44.667
That is easier to review than a raw pointer plus a separate length value that must stay correct through every branch.

11
00:00:44.667 --> 00:00:45.813
Initialization discipline matters.

12
00:00:45.813 --> 00:00:51.157
Allocated storage should become a valid object before it is exposed to other code.

13
00:00:51.157 --> 00:00:58.411
Constructors, factories, and RAII wrappers should establish invariants before callbacks, output parameters, global state, or other observers can see

14
00:00:58.411 --> 00:00:59.175
the object.

15
00:00:59.175 --> 00:01:01.465
Partially constructed resources should not escape.

16
00:01:01.465 --> 00:01:07.955
If construction fails halfway through, cleanup should happen inside the owner that knows what has been initialized.

17
00:01:07.955 --> 00:01:13.300
Other code should not receive an object that requires guessing which parts are valid.

18
00:01:13.300 --> 00:01:17.118
Define failure behavior when allocation or construction cannot safely complete.

19
00:01:17.118 --> 00:01:26.281
The policy might be an error return, an exception, a rejected request, a smaller operation, or a fail-closed path according to project standards.

20
00:01:26.281 --> 00:01:30.480
What matters is that incomplete construction does not create ambiguous ownership.
